            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Epictetus</strong><br>\nEpictetus was born around 50 AD in Hierapolis, Phrygia, which is now part of modern-day Turkey. As a young boy, he was born into slavery, serving in the household of Epaphroditos, a wealthy freedman and secretary to Nero. Despite his enslavement, Epictetus was able to study stoic philosophy, which laid the foundation for his later teachings and writings. His physical condition was challenging as he was said to have been lame in one leg.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Studying Under Musonius Rufus</strong><br>\nWhile still a slave, Epictetus began to study philosophy under the tutelage of Musonius Rufus, one of the most prominent Stoic philosophers of the time. Rufus's teachings profoundly influenced Epictetus, emphasizing that philosophy is not just theoretical knowledge but a way of life that demands practical application and personal discipline.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy as a Way of Life</strong><br>\nEpictetus's philosophy centered around the idea that individuals are responsible for their own happiness and that this happiness depends on their inner life, not their external circumstances. He believed that while we cannot control external events, we can control our perceptions and attitudes towards them, which is the basis for inner peace and happiness.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
