            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Concept of Freedom</strong><br>\nEpictetus taught that true freedom comes from understanding the limits of our own power. He argued that most people are enslaved by their desires and aversions, but a person can be free only when they let go of things beyond their control. According to Epictetus, freedom is achieved by living in harmony with nature and accepting the present moment as it is.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Art of Living</strong><br>\nFor Epictetus, the 'art of living' was like any other skill that needed to be learned and practiced. He emphasized the importance of self-examination and discipline as the pathways to achieving a good life. His teachings encouraged followers to live simply, to be self-sufficient, and to focus on one's own moral progress.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Enchiridion and Discourses</strong><br>\nAlthough Epictetus wrote nothing himself, his teachings were recorded by his pupil, Arrian, in two works: the 'Enchiridion,' a manual on Stoic ethics, and the 'Discourses.' These texts are crucial sources of stoic philosophy and offer practical guidance on how to live a virtuous life in accordance with nature.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
