          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Epictetus's Influence</strong><br>\nEpictetus's teachings have had a lasting impact, influencing both his contemporaries and later generations. His work played a significant role in the development of Roman Stoicism and continued to be read and admired by Christian theologians, Renaissance thinkers, and modern philosophers alike.</p>\n\n<p><strong>His School in Nicopolis</strong><br>\nAfter being granted his freedom, Epictetus founded a philosophy school in Nicopolis, Greece. There, he spent the rest of his life teaching Stoic philosophy to many students, including some of the most prominent citizens and future leaders of the time. His school attracted students from across the Roman Empire, drawn by the fame of his wisdom and teachings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nEpictetus lived during the reigns of several Roman emperors, witnessing significant changes and tumults in the empire. He died around 135 AD, leaving behind a philosophical legacy that emphasized ethics, personal freedom, and human dignity. Despite facing the challenge of physical disability and the limitations of his early life as a slave, his influence persists to this day, demonstrating the enduring power of Stoic principles.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
